e  and then you get some flourishes with  the a you pulled it down there just kind  of scoop underneath both of you  I like that yeah it looks amazing to me  let's look at the third piece what are  we doing here just having fun this is a  brush it's like us it's the studio pen  brush that I slightly modified as well  we're usually tapers slightly at the end  yeah I took the paper out so is the  brush itself does it make that teardrop  shape and you're just drawing it all  right yeah I mean with it with a certain  amount  yes Wow so each one of these teardrop  shapes is a brush stroke right you just  pull it across  is there a mass that's holding back the  Jo Y or is it just you're just staying  within the line no so I had already  drawn the Jo I so I just did it in a  slightly different color and built  around it basically kind of cushioning a  little bit more every time but then  whatever color I'm working in say it's  I'm probably drawing red right now hmm  I would like kind of create a ring of  red and then leave the red into open  spaces and the colors before it so just  making sure everything's kind of evenly  distributed and usually I do that by  color coding so especially when I'm  working on a really complex piece yes  what I have like the same leaf woven in  through a pattern 20 times just in order  to make sure that I'm actually getting  an equal distribution I'll draw in a  completely different color so that I can  tell where I'm putting them whap it to  black and white like as I finish it okay  guys I think we're kind of at the tail  end of this you guys make sure you guys  follow at little patterns that's  Maggie's account that's Maggie into Rios  little patterns and calm and that's it  so Maggie thank you very much for coming  on the show guys thank you  [Applause]

